libsanitizer: let LeakSanitizer run on mips32

CAN_SANITIZE_LEAKS was 1 for mips64 -- via the wordsize arm -- and 0 for
32-bit mips, which fell through to the #else. Nothing in the leak scanner is
64-bit specific: it needs a stack pointer per suspended thread, which
sanitizer_stoptheworld_linux_libcdep.cpp supplies for mips, and a
pointer-aligned sweep of the mapped ranges, which is wordsize-agnostic. The
exclusion looks like an omission rather than a limitation, so it is lifted and
tested rather than trusted.

Verified on an Ingenic t31 (mipsel o32, musl 1.2.4, gcc 13.3): a planted leak
is reported with a symbolised file:line trace, from the main thread and from
live spawned threads, and a program with no leak reports none. Before this the
runtime answered "detect_leaks is not supported on this platform".

Needs the ptrace-stack-pointer, unwinder-reentry, thread-descriptor,
dtv-offset and time-t-abi patches to be useful; on its own it runs, but the
answers are wrong.

Signed-off-by: OpenIPC AI team <ai@openipc.org>

--- a/libsanitizer/lsan/lsan_common.h
+++ b/libsanitizer/lsan/lsan_common.h
@@ -40,7 +40,17 @@
 #elif defined(__i386__) && (SANITIZER_LINUX || SANITIZER_MAC)
 #define CAN_SANITIZE_LEAKS 1
 #elif defined(__arm__) && SANITIZER_LINUX
-#define CAN_SANITIZE_LEAKS 1
+#  define CAN_SANITIZE_LEAKS 1
+#elif defined(__mips__) && SANITIZER_LINUX
+// 32-bit mips. The 64-bit ABIs are already covered by the __mips64 arm of the
+// wordsize test above; this arm is what o32 and n32 fall through to. Nothing
+// in the leak scanner is 64-bit specific -- it needs a stack pointer per
+// suspended thread, which sanitizer_stoptheworld_linux_libcdep.cpp supplies
+// for mips, and a pointer-aligned sweep of the mapped ranges, which is
+// wordsize-agnostic. Verified on an Ingenic t31 (mipsel o32, musl): a planted
+// leak is reported with a symbolised trace, from the main thread and from
+// live spawned threads.
+#  define CAN_SANITIZE_LEAKS 1
 #elif SANITIZER_NETBSD || SANITIZER_FUCHSIA
 #define CAN_SANITIZE_LEAKS 1
 #else
